<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osei Bonsu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1779">1779</a>–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Osei Tutu Kwame</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Osei Bonsu, also known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> Kwame, was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a>, King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a>, from either 1800 or 1804 until his death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> in 1824. His name means whale in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a>, a symbol of his extension of the Ashanti Empire to the coast. Early in his reign he suppressed a rebellion of Muslim subjects in the north-west who wanted to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-kwame-panyin">Osei Kwame Panyin</a>. He led wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> in 1806–07 and against Gyaman in 1818–19, and the Ashanti came to dominate the Gold Coast trade. He rebuilt the great roads, began the Aban Palace in Kumasi, and brought literate Muslims into the Ashanti bureaucracy.</p></div>
